#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Gary Frattarola <garyf@parkviewlab.ai>
# SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later
#
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.11"
# dependencies = ["anthropic==1.6.0"]
# ///
"""Generate a release's changelog section from its history and its pull requests.

Usage:
    generate-changelog [--mode generate|insert|both] [--tag vX.Y.Z] [--repo DIR] [--reuse-committed]

One script for every ParkviewLab release workflow, run from a checkout of
dev-tools at a pinned release. It reports on the repository at --repo (default:
the current directory), which must be the root of its git checkout; it never
takes the repository from its own location, so a dev-tools checkout inside the
workspace is not read. The only thing it reads from its own checkout is its own
version (VERSION.txt), which it prints. The repository's GitHub address is read
from the origin remote, else from GITHUB_REPOSITORY.

Modes
-----
  generate  Read the history at the tag and the repository's merged pull requests,
            ask the model for the Highlights paragraph, and write the complete
            section to release-body.md at the repository root.
  insert    Put release-body.md into CHANGELOG.md below "## [Unreleased]",
            creating the file when it is absent. No network. When CHANGELOG.md
            already holds a section for the tag, it does nothing and says so, so
            a re-run after a partial failure never adds a second section.
  both      generate, then insert (the default).

The tag comes from --tag, else from GITHUB_REF on a tag push; with neither,
generate refuses and exits with status 2. --tag serves every run outside a tag
push: a local run, a dry run, and the repair of a failed release.

With --reuse-committed, which the release workflow passes, generate first looks
in CHANGELOG.md on origin/main: when a section for the tag is already there, it
writes that section to release-body.md unchanged, reads nothing from GitHub,
makes no model call, and says so. A re-run of a release job whose section was
committed before the job failed therefore creates the Release from the
committed text, and the two published forms agree. Without the option the
section is always built afresh.

The section
-----------
    ## [vX.Y.Z] - YYYY-MM-DD      (the tagged commit's date)

    ### Highlights

    <a paragraph written by the model>

    <the list>

The list is built by seven rules (R1 to R7), and the points they decide (D1 to
D18) are marked in the comments below:

  R1  The range is git rev-list <previous>..<tag>, where the previous tag is the
      highest vX.Y.Z tag below this one by its three numbers (D1); a first
      release's range is the whole history, git rev-list <tag> (D2).
  R2  A commit belongs to pull request #N when it is (a) a squash commit made by
      GitHub: committer noreply@github.com, one parent, a first line ending in
      (#N), the last such suffix counting (D4); (b) a merge made by GitHub whose
      first line ends in (#N) or reads "Merge pull request #N from ..."; or (c)
      a cherry-pick of one of those: a commit, not a merge, whose "(cherry
      picked from commit X)" trailer names one, or, failing a trailer, whose
      patch-id equals one's (D6). Commits of kinds (a) and (b) are looked for
      in every ref of the clone (D3). A real merge's own commits, those
      reachable from its second parent and not its first, belong to its pull
      request and are never listed on their own, except another pull
      request's commit of kind (a) or (b) among them (D5). A commit ending in
      (#N) that is none of these is not believed: it is a direct commit, and
      the script warns.
  R3  A pull request from this repository, not a fork, whose head branch is
      develop, main or back-merge-vX.Y.Z carries work recorded elsewhere and
      is not listed (D9). Its merge is examined under R5 (iii) and its other
      commits one by one (D10); a carrier merged by squash is left out with a
      warning. Carriers are left out of the patch-id index (D7).
  R4  A pull request is not listed when a commit attributed to it is reachable
      from the previous tag (D11), or when it was merged for real and every
      one of its own commits other than merges has a patch-id among the
      commits reachable from the previous tag (D8).
  R5  Every other commit in the range is bookkeeping, left out, or a direct
      commit, listed. Bookkeeping is recognised by what a commit changes,
      never by its title: (i) a commit, not a merge, whose diff apart from
      CHANGELOG.md changes only the project's own version value (D12); (ii) a
      commit, not a merge, that changes only the root CHANGELOG.md, a root
      commit compared with the empty tree (D13); (iii) a merge not made by
      GitHub, or a carrier's merge, whose tree equals the automatic merge of
      its first two parents (D15); (iv) a commit that changes nothing (D14).
  R6  A pull request's title is the first line of its squash commit or titled
      merge without the (#N) suffix; for a merge at GitHub's default title, the
      title GitHub records (D17). A title "type(scope)!: description" is
      grouped by its type, matched without regard to case (D16); any other
      title goes under Other changes, shown in full.
  R7  A pull request is breaking when its title has "!" after the type, or a
      line beginning "BREAKING CHANGE:" or "BREAKING-CHANGE:" appears in its
      squash, merge or pick message, in one of its own commits, or in its body
      on GitHub (D18). It is listed once, under Breaking changes. The script
      warns when the major number rises and nothing breaking is listed, never
      for a first release.

A pull request's line reads "- <description, first letter capitalised> (#N)";
a direct commit's reads "- <subject> (<short hash>)". The groups appear in the
order Breaking changes, Features, Bug fixes, Performance, Refactor, Docs, Tests,
Reverts, Maintenance, Other changes, Direct commits; an empty group is omitted,
and a release with nothing to list reads "_No changes._".

GitHub and the model
--------------------
The script reads one thing from GitHub's REST API, through gh (with GH_TOKEN in
a workflow, the person's own login locally): the repository's merged pull
requests, with each one's number, title, body, base and head branch, head
repository and merge commit. If that read fails, the run fails and names the
cause: a list built without it would be wrong without saying so.

The Highlights paragraph, two to four sentences as the prompt asks, is written
by claude-opus-5 from the entries the list holds: the title and body of every
listed pull request, as GitHub records them, and the subject and body of every
direct commit. Every entry's title or subject goes in first and is never
shortened, so that no entry is lost to the input's cap of 50,000 characters;
the bodies share what remains, each shortened as needed and marked
[shortened], or left out when its share cannot hold the mark and some of its
text. When the titles and subjects alone exceed the cap, they are all sent,
without bodies, and the input exceeds it. A missing ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, a
failed call, a refusal, an empty answer or a stop at max_tokens gives the
marked placeholder, and the release ships; a warning in the job summary names
the cause. The anthropic SDK is imported inside that call alone, so everything
else, the tests included, needs the standard library only.

The job summary
---------------
The script prints a report, and appends it to GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY when that is
set: its own dev-tools version, the range, every listed pull request with the
route by which it was recognised, the carriers and the pull requests already
shipped that it left out, the bookkeeping commits with their reasons, and its
warnings. Warnings never enter the notes and never fail the run.

The inline metadata asks for Python 3.11 or later: the version files are read
with tomllib, which Python 3.11 added (the SDK itself needs 3.10).

Exit codes:
  0  success, the placeholder paragraph included
  1  a failure while running: git, the GitHub read, an unreadable release-body.md
  2  bad arguments: no tag, a tag that is not vX.Y.Z or does not exist, --repo
     not the root of a git checkout, release-body.md missing for insert
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import difflib
import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import tomllib
from dataclasses import dataclass, field
from pathlib import Path

PROG = "generate-changelog"

# The Highlights call. The model is pinned here, once for every repository;
# moving it is a dev-tools release. Thinking is left at the model's default
# (adaptive, on for claude-opus-5), which counts against max_tokens.
HIGHLIGHTS_MODEL = "claude-opus-5"
HIGHLIGHTS_EFFORT = "high"
HIGHLIGHTS_MAX_TOKENS = 16_000
MAX_INPUT_CHARS = 50_000
SHORTENED = " [shortened]"
PLACEHOLDER = "_Highlights were not generated for this release._"

RELEASE_BODY = "release-body.md"
CHANGELOG = "CHANGELOG.md"
CHANGELOG_SKELETON = "# Changelog\n\nAll notable changes to this project are recorded here.\n\n## [Unreleased]\n"

GITHUB_EMAIL = "noreply@github.com"
TAG_RE = re.compile(r"^v(\d+)\.(\d+)\.(\d+)$")
PR_SUFFIX = re.compile(r"\(#(\d+)\)\s*$")
MERGE_DEFAULT = re.compile(r"^Merge pull request #(\d+) from \S+")
PICK_TRAILER = re.compile(r"\(cherry picked from commit ([0-9a-f]{7,40})\)")
# D16: a type of letters only, an optional (scope), an optional !, a colon, at
# least one blank, and a description; the type is matched without regard to case.
CONVENTIONAL = re.compile(
    r"^(?P<type>[A-Za-z]+)(?:\((?P<scope>[^()\r\n]*)\))?(?P<bang>!)?:[ \t]+(?P<desc>\S.*)$"
)
# D18: at the start of a line, case-sensitive.
BREAKING_LINE = re.compile(r"(?m)^BREAKING[ -]CHANGE:")
# D9: the head branches of a carrier, from this repository only.
CARRIER_HEADS = ("develop", "main")
BACK_MERGE_HEAD = re.compile(r"^back-merge-v[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+$")
REMOTE_RE = re.compile(r"github\.com[:/](?P<owner>[^/:]+)/(?P<name>[^/]+?)(?:\.git)?/?$")
SECTION_HEAD_RE = re.compile(r"^## \[(v\d+\.\d+\.\d+)\]")

GROUP_OF_TYPE = {
    "feat": "Features",
    "fix": "Bug fixes",
    "perf": "Performance",
    "refactor": "Refactor",
    "docs": "Docs",
    "test": "Tests",
    "revert": "Reverts",
    "build": "Maintenance",
    "chore": "Maintenance",
    "ci": "Maintenance",
    "style": "Maintenance",
}
GROUP_ORDER = (
    "Breaking changes",
    "Features",
    "Bug fixes",
    "Performance",
    "Refactor",
    "Docs",
    "Tests",
    "Reverts",
    "Maintenance",
    "Other changes",
)

# D12: the version sources of truth and their lockfiles, at the repository root only.
SOT_FILES = frozenset({"pyproject.toml", "package.json", "Cargo.toml", "VERSION.txt"})
LOCK_FILES = frozenset({"uv.lock", "package-lock.json", "Cargo.lock"})
VERSION_LINE = re.compile(r'^\s*"?version"?\s*[:=]')

ROUTES = {
    "squash": "squash commit",
    "merge-titled": "merge titled with the pull request",
    "merge-default-title": "merge at GitHub's default title",
    "cherry-pick (trailer)": "cherry-pick, by its trailer",
    "cherry-pick (patch-id)": "cherry-pick, by patch-id",
}


class ChangelogError(Exception):
    """A failure that stops the run (exit status 1)."""


class UsageError(Exception):
    """Bad arguments (exit status 2)."""


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# The repository and its pull requests
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Repository:
    root: Path
    slug: str | None  # owner/name on GitHub, when it can be told

    @property
    def name(self) -> str:
        return self.slug.split("/", 1)[1] if self.slug else self.root.name


def resolve_repository(arg: str) -> Repository:
    root = Path(arg).resolve()
    if not root.is_dir():
        raise UsageError(f"--repo {arg}: not a directory")
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(
            ["git", "-C", str(root), "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"], capture_output=True, text=True, check=False
        )
    except OSError as e:
        raise ChangelogError(f"git cannot be run: {e}") from None
    if p.returncode != 0:
        raise UsageError(f"--repo {arg}: not a git checkout")
    top = Path(p.stdout.strip()).resolve()
    if top != root:
        raise UsageError(f"--repo {arg} is inside the checkout at {top} but is not its root; pass the root")
    url = subprocess.run(
        ["git", "-C", str(root), "remote", "get-url", "origin"], capture_output=True, text=True, check=False
    )
    m = REMOTE_RE.search(url.stdout.strip()) if url.returncode == 0 else None
    if m:
        return Repository(root, f"{m['owner']}/{m['name']}")
    slug = os.environ.get("GITHUB_REPOSITORY", "")
    if slug.count("/") == 1 and all(slug.split("/")):
        return Repository(root, slug)
    return Repository(root, None)


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class PullRequest:
    number: int
    title: str
    body: str
    base: str
    head: str
    same_repository: bool  # the head branch lives in this repository, not a fork
    merge_commit: str | None


def pull_request_from_rest(obj: dict) -> PullRequest | None:
    """A merged pull request from one object of GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls; None when unmerged."""
    if not obj.get("merged_at"):
        return None
    head = obj.get("head") or {}
    base = obj.get("base") or {}
    head_repo = (head.get("repo") or {}).get("full_name")
    base_repo = (base.get("repo") or {}).get("full_name")
    # A deleted fork leaves head.repo null; it is still not this repository.
    same = bool(head_repo and base_repo and head_repo.lower() == base_repo.lower())
    return PullRequest(
        number=int(obj["number"]),
        title=obj.get("title") or "",
        body=obj.get("body") or "",
        base=base.get("ref") or "",
        head=head.get("ref") or "",
        same_repository=same,
        merge_commit=obj.get("merge_commit_sha"),
    )


def fetch_pull_requests(slug: str) -> list[dict]:
    """Every closed pull request of the repository, as GitHub's REST API returns them."""
    gh = shutil.which("gh")
    if not gh:
        raise ChangelogError("gh (the GitHub CLI) is not on PATH; it reads the repository's pull requests")
    cmd = [gh, "api", "--paginate", f"repos/{slug}/pulls?state=closed&per_page=100"]
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True, errors="replace", check=False)
    except OSError as e:
        raise ChangelogError(f"gh cannot be run: {e}") from None
    if p.returncode != 0:
        raise ChangelogError(
            f"reading the pull requests of {slug} from GitHub failed (gh api exited {p.returncode}): "
            f"{p.stderr.strip() or p.stdout.strip()}"
        )
    return parse_pages(p.stdout, slug)


def parse_pages(text: str, slug: str) -> list[dict]:
    """gh api --paginate prints one JSON array per page, one after another."""
    decoder = json.JSONDecoder()
    items: list[dict] = []
    i = 0
    while True:
        while i < len(text) and text[i].isspace():
            i += 1
        if i >= len(text):
            return items
        try:
            page, i = decoder.raw_decode(text, i)
        except json.JSONDecodeError as e:
            raise ChangelogError(f"the pull requests of {slug} read from GitHub are not JSON: {e}") from None
        if not isinstance(page, list):
            raise ChangelogError(f"the pull requests of {slug} read from GitHub are not a list")
        items.extend(page)


def read_pull_requests(repo: Repository) -> dict[int, PullRequest]:
    if not repo.slug:
        raise ChangelogError(
            "cannot tell which GitHub repository this is: no github.com origin remote to read, "
            "and GITHUB_REPOSITORY is not set"
        )
    prs: dict[int, PullRequest] = {}
    for obj in fetch_pull_requests(repo.slug):
        pr = pull_request_from_rest(obj)
        if pr is not None:
            prs[pr.number] = pr
    return prs


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# git
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #


class Git:
    def __init__(self, root: Path) -> None:
        self.root = root
        # A signature or colour shown by a person's configuration would break the parsing:
        # a coloured diff gives git patch-id nothing to read. color.diff, not color.ui,
        # since a person's color.diff=always outranks color.ui=false.
        self.base = ["git", "-C", str(root), "-c", "log.showSignature=false", "-c", "color.diff=false"]

    def run(self, *args: str, check: bool = True) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess[str]:
        try:
            p = subprocess.run([*self.base, *args], capture_output=True, text=True, errors="replace", check=False)
        except OSError as e:
            raise ChangelogError(f"git cannot be run: {e}") from None
        if check and p.returncode != 0:
            raise ChangelogError(f"git {' '.join(args)} failed: {p.stderr.strip()}")
        return p

    def out(self, *args: str) -> str:
        return self.run(*args).stdout

    def raw(self, *args: str, stdin: bytes | None = None) -> bytes:
        """Binary-safe, for the diffs piped into git patch-id."""
        try:
            p = subprocess.run([*self.base, *args], input=stdin, capture_output=True, check=False)
        except OSError as e:
            raise ChangelogError(f"git cannot be run: {e}") from None
        if p.returncode != 0:
            raise ChangelogError(f"git {' '.join(args)} failed: {p.stderr.decode(errors='replace').strip()}")
        return p.stdout

    def blob(self, rev: str, path: str) -> str | None:
        """A file's text at a revision, or None when it is absent there."""
        p = self.run("cat-file", "blob", f"{rev}:{path}", check=False)
        return p.stdout if p.returncode == 0 else None


def version_key(tag: str) -> tuple[int, int, int]:
    m = TAG_RE.match(tag)
    if not m:
        raise ValueError(tag)
    return (int(m[1]), int(m[2]), int(m[3]))


def previous_tag(git: Git, tag: str) -> str | None:
    """D1: the highest vX.Y.Z tag below this one, by its three numbers."""
    key = version_key(tag)
    below = [t for t in git.out("tag", "--list", "v*").split() if TAG_RE.match(t) and version_key(t) < key]
    return max(below, key=version_key) if below else None


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# The history index (R2)
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Commit:
    sha: str
    parents: tuple[str, ...]
    email: str
    subject: str


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Attribution:
    route: str  # a key of ROUTES
    number: int
    original: str | None = None  # the GitHub-made commit a cherry-pick copies


class History:
    """Every commit reachable from any ref of the clone: branches, remote-tracking
    branches and tags (D3), with GitHub's commits attributed to pull requests."""

    def __init__(self, git: Git, prs: dict[int, PullRequest]) -> None:
        self.git = git
        self.prs = prs
        self.commits: dict[str, Commit] = {}
        for rec in git.out("log", "--all", "--format=%H%x1f%P%x1f%ce%x1f%s%x1e").split("\x1e"):
            rec = rec.strip("\n")
            if not rec:
                continue
            sha, parents, email, subject = rec.split("\x1f", 3)
            # git's %s is one line, but a lone CR in it reaches here as a line feed, since
            # text mode translates line endings; it is read as a space, as git joins the
            # subject's lines.
            self.commits[sha] = Commit(sha, tuple(parents.split()), email, subject.replace("\n", " "))
        self._messages: dict[str, str] | None = None
        self._pid: dict[str, str | None] = {}
        self._load_patch_ids()
        # R2 (a) and (b): GitHub-made commits attributed to a pull request, anywhere.
        self.github: dict[str, Attribution] = {}
        for sha, c in self.commits.items():
            a = self._github_attribution(c)
            if a is not None:
                self.github[sha] = a
        # R2 (c): patch-id -> pull requests, over GitHub's commits of pull requests
        # that are not carriers, so no commit is taken for a pick of a carrier (D7).
        self.pid_index: dict[str, set[int]] = {}
        for sha, a in self.github.items():
            if self.is_carrier(a.number):
                continue
            pid = self.patch_id(sha)
            if pid:
                self.pid_index.setdefault(pid, set()).add(a.number)

    def is_merge(self, sha: str) -> bool:
        return len(self.commits[sha].parents) >= 2

    def is_carrier(self, n: int) -> bool:
        """R3 (D9): by head branch alone, and only from this repository."""
        pr = self.prs.get(n)
        if pr is None or not pr.same_repository:
            return False
        return pr.head in CARRIER_HEADS or bool(BACK_MERGE_HEAD.match(pr.head))

    def message(self, sha: str) -> str:
        if self._messages is None:
            self._messages = {}
            for rec in self.git.out("log", "--all", "--format=%H%x1f%B%x1e").split("\x1e"):
                rec = rec.lstrip("\n")
                if "\x1f" in rec:
                    s, body = rec.split("\x1f", 1)
                    self._messages[s] = body
        if sha not in self._messages:
            self._messages[sha] = self.git.out("log", "-1", "--format=%B", sha)
        return self._messages[sha]

    def body(self, sha: str) -> str:
        """The message without its subject paragraph."""
        parts = self.message(sha).strip("\n").split("\n\n", 1)
        return parts[1].strip() if len(parts) == 2 else ""

    def _github_attribution(self, c: Commit) -> Attribution | None:
        if c.email != GITHUB_EMAIL:
            return None
        if len(c.parents) >= 2:
            m = MERGE_DEFAULT.match(c.subject)
            if m:
                return Attribution("merge-default-title", int(m[1]))
            m = PR_SUFFIX.search(c.subject)
            return Attribution("merge-titled", int(m[1])) if m else None
        if len(c.parents) == 1:  # D4: one parent, the last (#N) of the first line
            m = PR_SUFFIX.search(c.subject)
            return Attribution("squash", int(m[1])) if m else None
        return None

    def _load_patch_ids(self) -> None:
        """D6: git patch-id --stable of every commit that is not a merge, from one log pass."""
        log = self.git.raw(
            "log", "--all", "--no-merges", "--no-renames", "--no-textconv", "--no-ext-diff", "-p", "--format=commit %H"
        )
        out = self.git.raw("patch-id", "--stable", stdin=log).decode()
        for line in out.splitlines():
            pid, sha = line.split()
            self._pid[sha] = pid

    def patch_id(self, sha: str) -> str | None:
        """A commit's own diff; for a merge (D6), git diff M^1 M, which git cherry-pick -m 1 M applies."""
        if sha in self._pid:
            return self._pid[sha]
        pid = None
        if self.is_merge(sha):
            diff = self.git.raw("diff", "--no-renames", "--no-textconv", "--no-ext-diff", f"{sha}^1", sha)
            if diff:
                out = self.git.raw("patch-id", "--stable", stdin=diff).decode().split()
                pid = out[0] if out else None
        self._pid[sha] = pid  # an empty commit has none
        return pid

    def attribute(self, sha: str) -> Attribution | None:
        """R2 (a, b, c) for one commit."""
        if sha in self.github:
            return self.github[sha]
        if self.is_merge(sha):
            return None  # D6: a merge is never a cherry-pick
        for x in PICK_TRAILER.findall(self.message(sha)):  # D6: the trailer first
            p = self.git.run("rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", f"{x}^{{commit}}", check=False)
            full = p.stdout.strip()
            if p.returncode == 0 and full in self.github and not self.is_carrier(self.github[full].number):
                return Attribution("cherry-pick (trailer)", self.github[full].number, full)
        pid = self.patch_id(sha)
        if pid and pid in self.pid_index:
            n = min(self.pid_index[pid])  # D6: several pull requests share it: the lowest number
            original = next(s for s, a in self.github.items() if a.number == n and self.patch_id(s) == pid)
            return Attribution("cherry-pick (patch-id)", n, original)
        return None


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Bookkeeping (R5)
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #


def _norm(name: str) -> str:
    return re.sub(r"[-_.]+", "-", name).lower()


def _without(d: dict, *path: str) -> dict:
    """A deep copy of d with the key at path removed."""
    d = json.loads(json.dumps(d, default=str))
    node = d
    for key in path[:-1]:
        node = node.get(key)
        if not isinstance(node, dict):
            return d
    node.pop(path[-1], None)
    return d


def _package_lock_without_version(d: dict) -> dict:
    d = _without(d, "version")
    packages = d.get("packages")
    if isinstance(packages, dict) and isinstance(packages.get(""), dict):
        packages[""].pop("version", None)
    return d


def _toml_lock_without_own_version(d: dict, own_names: set[str]) -> dict:
    d = json.loads(json.dumps(d, default=str))
    for pkg in d.get("package", []) or []:
        if not isinstance(pkg, dict):
            continue
        src = pkg.get("source")
        own = isinstance(src, dict) and (src.get("editable") == "." or src.get("virtual") == ".")
        if own or _norm(str(pkg.get("name", ""))) in own_names:
            pkg.pop("version", None)
    return d


def _only_version_lines_change(old: str, new: str) -> bool:
    a, b = old.splitlines(), new.splitlines()
    for op, i1, i2, j1, j2 in difflib.SequenceMatcher(a=a, b=b, autojunk=False).get_opcodes():
        if op != "equal" and not all(VERSION_LINE.match(line) for line in a[i1:i2] + b[j1:j2]):
            return False
    return True


def version_only_change(git: Git, parent: str, sha: str, path: str) -> tuple[bool, str]:
    """D12: every changed line a version line, and the parsed file otherwise identical."""
    old, new = git.blob(parent, path), git.blob(sha, path)
    if old is None or new is None:
        return False, f"{path} added or removed"
    if path == "VERSION.txt":
        ok = bool(re.fullmatch(r"\s*\S+\s*", old)) and bool(re.fullmatch(r"\s*\S+\s*", new))
        return (ok, f"VERSION.txt {old.strip()} -> {new.strip()}") if ok else (False, "VERSION.txt not a single value")
    if not _only_version_lines_change(old, new):
        return False, f"{path}: a changed line is not a version line"
    try:
        if path == "pyproject.toml":
            a, b = tomllib.loads(old), tomllib.loads(new)
            same = _without(a, "project", "version") == _without(b, "project", "version")
            va, vb = (a.get("project") or {}).get("version"), (b.get("project") or {}).get("version")
        elif path == "Cargo.toml":
            a, b = tomllib.loads(old), tomllib.loads(new)
            same = _without(a, "package", "version") == _without(b, "package", "version")
            va, vb = (a.get("package") or {}).get("version"), (b.get("package") or {}).get("version")
        elif path == "package.json":
            a, b = json.loads(old), json.loads(new)
            same = _without(a, "version") == _without(b, "version")
            va, vb = a.get("version"), b.get("version")
        elif path == "package-lock.json":
            a, b = json.loads(old), json.loads(new)
            same = _package_lock_without_version(a) == _package_lock_without_version(b)
            va, vb = a.get("version"), b.get("version")
        elif path in ("uv.lock", "Cargo.lock"):
            manifest = "pyproject.toml" if path == "uv.lock" else "Cargo.toml"
            own: set[str] = set()
            for rev in (parent, sha):
                text = git.blob(rev, manifest)
                if not text:
                    continue
                try:
                    m = tomllib.loads(text)
                except tomllib.TOMLDecodeError:
                    continue
                name = (m.get("project") or m.get("package") or {}).get("name")
                if name:
                    own.add(_norm(str(name)))
            a, b = tomllib.loads(old), tomllib.loads(new)
            same = _toml_lock_without_own_version(a, own) == _toml_lock_without_own_version(b, own)
            if not same:
                return False, f"{path}: a value other than the project's own version changed"
            return True, f"{path} own entry"
        else:
            return False, f"{path} is not a version file"
    except (tomllib.TOMLDecodeError, json.JSONDecodeError, AttributeError) as e:
        return False, f"{path} does not parse: {e}"
    if not same:
        return False, f"{path}: a value other than the project's own version changed"
    return True, f"{path} {va} -> {vb}"


def bookkeeping_of(git: Git, sha: str, parents: tuple[str, ...]) -> tuple[str | None, str]:
    """R5 (i), (ii) and (iv) for a commit that is not a merge: (reason or None, detail)."""
    if parents:
        out = git.out("diff-tree", "-r", "-z", "--no-commit-id", "--no-renames", "--name-status", parents[0], sha)
    else:  # D13: a root commit is compared with the empty tree
        out = git.out("diff-tree", "-r", "-z", "--no-commit-id", "--no-renames", "--name-status", "--root", sha)
    fields = out.split("\0")
    changes = [(fields[i], fields[i + 1]) for i in range(0, len(fields) - 1, 2) if fields[i]]
    if not changes:
        return "changes nothing", "an empty commit"  # D14
    other = [(status, path) for status, path in changes if path != CHANGELOG]
    if not other:
        return "changelog only", f"changes only {CHANGELOG}"
    if not parents:
        return None, "root commit"
    details = []
    for status, path in other:
        if status != "M" or path not in SOT_FILES | LOCK_FILES:
            return None, f"changes {path}"
        ok, detail = version_only_change(git, parents[0], sha, path)
        if not ok:
            return None, detail
        details.append(detail)
    return "version only", "; ".join(details) + (f"; {CHANGELOG}" if len(other) < len(changes) else "")


def merge_against_automatic(git: Git, sha: str, parents: tuple[str, ...]) -> tuple[bool, str]:
    """R5 (iii), D15: does the merge's tree equal the automatic merge of its first two parents?"""
    if len(parents) != 2:
        return False, f"{len(parents)}-parent merge: no automatic merge to compare with"
    p = git.run("merge-tree", "--write-tree", "--no-messages", "--name-only", parents[0], parents[1], check=False)
    if p.returncode not in (0, 1):
        return False, f"the automatic merge could not be computed: {p.stderr.strip()}"
    lines = p.stdout.splitlines()
    auto = lines[0].strip() if lines else ""
    conflicted = p.returncode == 1
    tree = git.out("rev-parse", f"{sha}^{{tree}}").strip()
    if auto == tree and not conflicted:
        return True, "tree equals the automatic merge of its parents"
    stat = git.out("diff", "--no-renames", "--stat=200", auto, tree).strip() if auto else ""
    files = sorted({ln for ln in lines[1:] if ln.strip()})
    what = f"automatic merge conflicts in {', '.join(files)}; " if conflicted else ""
    return False, what + "differs from the automatic merge: " + (stat.replace("\n", " | ") or "(no textual diff)")


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# The rule
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #


@dataclass
class PullEntry:
    number: int
    commits: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)  # the commits in the range attributed to it
    routes: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
    originals: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)  # for a cherry-pick, the commit it copies
    own: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)  # a real merge's own commits in the range (D5)
    title: str = ""
    type: str | None = None
    breaking: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
    group: str = ""
    line: str = ""


@dataclass
class DirectCommit:
    sha: str
    subject: str
    body: str
    merge: bool
    note: str


@dataclass
class Bookkeeping:
    sha: str
    subject: str
    reason: str


@dataclass
class LeftOut:
    number: int
    why: str


@dataclass
class Result:
    tag: str
    prev: str | None
    commit_count: int
    groups: dict[str, list[PullEntry]]
    direct: list[DirectCommit]
    bookkeeping: list[Bookkeeping]
    carriers: list[LeftOut]
    shipped: list[LeftOut]
    warnings: list[str]

    @property
    def range_text(self) -> str:
        return f"{self.prev}..{self.tag}" if self.prev else f"{self.tag} (the whole history)"

    @property
    def listed(self) -> list[PullEntry]:
        return [e for g in GROUP_ORDER for e in self.groups.get(g, [])]

    def render(self) -> str:
        parts = []
        for g in GROUP_ORDER:
            entries = self.groups.get(g)
            if entries:
                lines = [f"- {e.line[:1].upper()}{e.line[1:]} (#{e.number})" for e in entries]
                parts.append(f"### {g}\n\n" + "\n".join(lines))
        if self.direct:
            parts.append("### Direct commits\n\n" + "\n".join(f"- {d.subject} ({d.sha[:7]})" for d in self.direct))
        return "\n\n".join(parts) if parts else "_No changes._"


@dataclass
class Conventional:
    type: str | None
    bang: bool
    desc: str


def conventional(title: str) -> Conventional:
    m = CONVENTIONAL.match(title.strip())
    if not m:
        return Conventional(None, False, title.strip())
    return Conventional(m["type"].lower(), bool(m["bang"]), m["desc"].strip())


def strip_suffix(subject: str) -> str:
    return PR_SUFFIX.sub("", subject).rstrip()


def build_list(git: Git, prs: dict[int, PullRequest], tag: str) -> Result:
    history = History(git, prs)
    prev = previous_tag(git, tag)
    warnings: list[str] = []

    # R1
    spec = [f"refs/tags/{prev}..refs/tags/{tag}"] if prev else [f"refs/tags/{tag}"]
    rng = git.out("rev-list", "--topo-order", "--reverse", *spec).split()
    in_range = set(rng)

    # D5: the own commits of each real merge of a listed pull request in the range.
    absorbed: dict[str, int] = {}
    own_commits: dict[int, list[str]] = {}
    for sha in rng:
        a = history.github.get(sha)
        if a is None or not a.route.startswith("merge") or history.is_carrier(a.number):
            continue
        parents = history.commits[sha].parents
        for c in git.out("rev-list", *parents[1:], f"^{parents[0]}").split():
            if c not in in_range:
                continue
            nested = history.github.get(c)
            if nested is not None and not history.is_carrier(nested.number):
                continue  # another pull request's own commit stays that pull request's
            absorbed.setdefault(c, a.number)
            own_commits.setdefault(a.number, []).append(c)

    listed: dict[int, PullEntry] = {}
    direct: list[DirectCommit] = []
    bookkeeping: list[Bookkeeping] = []
    carriers: dict[int, LeftOut] = {}

    def add(n: int, sha: str, a: Attribution) -> None:
        e = listed.setdefault(n, PullEntry(n))
        e.commits.append(sha)
        e.routes.append(a.route)
        if a.original:
            e.originals.append(a.original)
        if not a.route.startswith("cherry-pick"):  # the check against GitHub's record
            recorded = prs[n].merge_commit if n in prs else None
            if recorded and recorded != sha:
                warnings.append(f"{sha[:10]} claims #{n}, but GitHub records {recorded[:10]} as its merge commit")

    for sha in rng:
        if sha in absorbed:
            continue
        c = history.commits[sha]
        a = history.attribute(sha)
        if a is not None:
            if history.is_carrier(a.number):  # R3
                pr = prs[a.number]
                carriers.setdefault(a.number, LeftOut(a.number, f"carrier (head {pr.head}), {ROUTES[a.route]} {sha[:10]}"))
                if a.route == "squash":
                    warnings.append(
                        f"{sha[:10]} is a squash merge of carrier pull request #{a.number}; "
                        "the changes it carries cannot be told apart, so it is left out"
                    )
                    continue
                # D10: the carrier's merge is examined as if it were not there.
                ok, why = merge_against_automatic(git, sha, c.parents)
                if ok:
                    bookkeeping.append(Bookkeeping(sha, c.subject, f"carrier #{a.number}'s merge: {why}"))
                else:
                    direct.append(DirectCommit(sha, c.subject, history.body(sha), True, why))
                    warnings.append(f"{sha[:10]} carrier #{a.number}'s merge {why}")
                continue
            if a.number not in prs:
                warnings.append(f"{sha[:10]} is attributed to #{a.number}, which GitHub does not list as merged")
            add(a.number, sha, a)
            continue
        if len(c.parents) >= 2:  # R5 (iii)
            ok, why = merge_against_automatic(git, sha, c.parents)
            if ok:
                bookkeeping.append(Bookkeeping(sha, c.subject, f"merge: {why}"))
            else:
                direct.append(DirectCommit(sha, c.subject, history.body(sha), True, why))
                warnings.append(f"{sha[:10]} merge '{c.subject}' {why}")
            continue
        reason, detail = bookkeeping_of(git, sha, c.parents)  # R5 (i), (ii), (iv)
        if reason:
            bookkeeping.append(Bookkeeping(sha, c.subject, f"{reason}: {detail}"))
            continue
        m = PR_SUFFIX.search(c.subject)
        if m:
            warnings.append(
                f"{sha[:10]} ends in (#{m[1]}) but is neither made by GitHub nor a cherry-pick of a "
                "GitHub-made commit; listed as a direct commit"
            )
        direct.append(DirectCommit(sha, c.subject, history.body(sha), False, detail))

    # R4: once only.
    shipped: list[LeftOut] = []
    if prev:
        prev_commits = git.out("rev-list", f"refs/tags/{prev}").split()
        evidence: dict[int, str] = {}
        for sha in prev_commits:  # D11: the ancestry of the version-previous tag only
            a = history.attribute(sha)
            if a is not None and a.number not in evidence:
                evidence[a.number] = f"{sha[:10]} ({ROUTES[a.route]})"
        prev_pids: set[str] | None = None
        for n in sorted(listed):
            if n in evidence:
                shipped.append(LeftOut(n, f"already shipped: {evidence[n]} is reachable from {prev}"))
                continue
            own = [c for c in own_commits.get(n, []) if not history.is_merge(c)]
            if not own:
                continue
            if prev_pids is None:
                prev_pids = {history.patch_id(s) for s in prev_commits if not history.is_merge(s)} - {None}
            if all(history.patch_id(c) in prev_pids for c in own):  # D8
                shipped.append(
                    LeftOut(n, f"already shipped: each of its {len(own)} own commits has a patch-id among "
                               f"the commits reachable from {prev}")
                )
        for s in shipped:
            listed.pop(s.number)

    # R6 titles and groups, R7 breaking changes.
    groups: dict[str, list[PullEntry]] = {g: [] for g in GROUP_ORDER}
    for n in sorted(listed):
        e = listed[n]
        pr = prs.get(n)
        e.own = own_commits.get(n, [])
        source = next((s for s in e.commits if s in history.github), None) or next(iter(e.originals), None)
        if source is not None:
            if history.github[source].route == "merge-default-title":  # D17
                if pr is not None and pr.title:
                    title = pr.title
                else:
                    lines = history.body(source).splitlines()
                    title = lines[0] if lines else history.commits[source].subject
                    warnings.append(f"#{n}: a merge at GitHub's default title, and GitHub gives no title for it")
            else:
                title = strip_suffix(history.commits[source].subject)
        else:
            title = pr.title if pr is not None else ""
        e.title = title
        cc = conventional(title)
        e.type = cc.type
        messages = [history.message(s) for s in e.commits + e.own]
        if cc.bang:
            e.breaking.append("! in the title")
        if any(BREAKING_LINE.search(m) for m in messages):
            e.breaking.append("a BREAKING CHANGE footer in a commit message")
        if pr is not None and BREAKING_LINE.search(pr.body):
            e.breaking.append("a BREAKING CHANGE footer in the pull request's body")
        if e.breaking:
            e.group = "Breaking changes"
        elif cc.type in GROUP_OF_TYPE:
            e.group = GROUP_OF_TYPE[cc.type]
        else:
            e.group = "Other changes"
        e.line = title if e.group == "Other changes" or cc.type is None else cc.desc
        groups[e.group].append(e)

    if prev and version_key(tag)[0] > version_key(prev)[0] and not groups["Breaking changes"]:
        warnings.append(f"{tag} raises the major version and lists no breaking change")

    return Result(
        tag=tag,
        prev=prev,
        commit_count=len(rng),
        groups={g: v for g, v in groups.items() if v},
        direct=direct,
        bookkeeping=bookkeeping,
        carriers=sorted(carriers.values(), key=lambda x: x.number),
        shipped=shipped,
        warnings=warnings,
    )


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# The Highlights paragraph
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #


def highlight_entries(result: Result, prs: dict[int, PullRequest]) -> list[tuple[str, str, str]]:
    """(key, head, body) for every entry of the list, in the list's order."""
    entries = []
    for e in result.listed:
        pr = prs.get(e.number)
        title = pr.title if pr is not None and pr.title else e.title
        entries.append((f"#{e.number}", f"#{e.number} {title}", pr.body if pr is not None else ""))
    for d in result.direct:
        entries.append((d.sha[:7], f"{d.sha[:7]} {d.subject}", d.body))
    return entries


def share(available: int, lengths: list[int]) -> list[int]:
    """Divide available characters among bodies of these lengths: a body shorter than
    its fair share keeps its length, and what it leaves goes to the longer ones."""
    allot = [0] * len(lengths)
    if available <= 0:
        return allot
    left, remaining = available, len(lengths)
    for i in sorted(range(len(lengths)), key=lambda i: lengths[i]):
        allot[i] = min(lengths[i], left // remaining)
        left -= allot[i]
        remaining -= 1
    return allot


def highlights_input(entries: list[tuple[str, str, str]], cap: int = MAX_INPUT_CHARS) -> str:
    """Every entry's title or subject first, then the bodies, sharing what remains of the
    cap. The titles are never cut, so no entry is lost; should they alone exceed the
    cap, the bodies are left out."""
    heads = "\n".join(f"- {head}" for _, head, _ in entries) if entries else "(nothing is listed)"
    bodies = [(key, body.strip()) for key, _, body in entries if body.strip()]
    labels = [f"\n\n--- {key}\n" for key, _ in bodies]
    available = cap - len(heads) - sum(len(label) for label in labels)
    parts = [heads]
    for (_, body), label, k in zip(bodies, labels, share(available, [len(b) for _, b in bodies]), strict=True):
        if len(body) <= k:
            parts.append(label + body)
        elif k > len(SHORTENED):
            parts.append(label + body[: k - len(SHORTENED)].rstrip() + SHORTENED)
    return "".join(parts)


def highlights_prompt(name: str, description: str, tag: str, prev: str | None, changes: str) -> str:
    framing = f"This is the FIRST tagged release of {name}." if prev is None else f"This release ({tag}) follows {prev}."
    project = f"{name} — {description}" if description else name
    return (
        f"You are writing the 'Highlights' paragraph for the CHANGELOG entry of {name} {tag}.\n\n"
        f"Project: {project}\n"
        f"{framing}\n\n"
        "The changes in this release are below: first one line for each change (a pull request, by its "
        "number and title, or a commit made without a pull request, by its short hash and subject), then "
        "the description of each change, some shortened to fit. Write a TIGHT prose paragraph summarising "
        "what is most USER-VISIBLE in this release. Constraints:\n"
        "- Two or three sentences. Hard maximum: four sentences.\n"
        "- Prose only. No bullet points. No headings. No code fences.\n"
        "- Do not restate the version number in the prose.\n"
        "- Do not invent features that aren't in the changes below.\n"
        "- If the release is purely internal/maintenance, say so honestly in one sentence.\n"
        "- Plain factual tone. No marketing language ('exciting', 'powerful', 'seamless', etc).\n"
        "- For an initial release summarising a lot of foundational work, prefer compressing "
        "into one dense sentence over running long — better to undersell than to truncate.\n\n"
        f"=== CHANGES ===\n{changes}\n=== END OF CHANGES ===\n\n"
        "Output ONLY the paragraph, nothing else."
    )


def highlights(prompt: str, warnings: list[str]) -> tuple[str, str]:
    """(the paragraph, what wrote it). Every failure gives the placeholder and a warning;
    the release ships either way."""
    if not os.environ.get("ANTHROPIC_API_KEY"):
        warnings.append("ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is not set, so no Highlights call was made; the placeholder is used")
        return PLACEHOLDER, "the placeholder"
    try:
        import anthropic  # only here: the rest of the script needs the standard library alone
    except ImportError as e:
        warnings.append(f"the anthropic SDK cannot be imported ({e}); the placeholder is used")
        return PLACEHOLDER, "the placeholder"
    try:
        client = anthropic.Anthropic()  # the key is read from the environment by the SDK
        response = client.beta.messages.create(
            model=HIGHLIGHTS_MODEL,
            max_tokens=HIGHLIGHTS_MAX_TOKENS,
            output_config={"effort": HIGHLIGHTS_EFFORT},
            messages=[{"role": "user", "content": prompt}],
        )
    except Exception as e:  # any failure of the call: the placeholder, and the release ships
        warnings.append(f"the Highlights call failed ({type(e).__name__}: {e}); the placeholder is used")
        return PLACEHOLDER, "the placeholder"
    stop = getattr(response, "stop_reason", None)
    if stop == "refusal":  # checked before the content is read
        category = getattr(getattr(response, "stop_details", None), "category", None)
        warnings.append(
            f"the Highlights call was refused (stop_reason refusal, category {category or 'not given'}); "
            "the placeholder is used"
        )
        return PLACEHOLDER, "the placeholder"
    text = "".join(
        getattr(block, "text", "") for block in (getattr(response, "content", None) or [])
        if getattr(block, "type", None) == "text"
    ).strip()
    if stop == "max_tokens":  # a paragraph cut off at the cap is never published
        warnings.append(
            f"the Highlights call stopped at max_tokens ({HIGHLIGHTS_MAX_TOKENS}); "
            + ("its paragraph was cut short" if text else "it returned no text")
            + ", so the placeholder is used"
        )
        return PLACEHOLDER, "the placeholder"
    if stop not in ("end_turn", "stop_sequence"):
        warnings.append(f"the Highlights call stopped with stop_reason {stop}")
    if not text:
        warnings.append("the Highlights call returned no text; the placeholder is used")
        return PLACEHOLDER, "the placeholder"
    return text, str(getattr(response, "model", None) or HIGHLIGHTS_MODEL)


def project_meta(git: Git, tag: str, fallback_name: str) -> tuple[str, str]:
    """The project's name and description from its manifest at the tag, else the repository's name."""
    readers = (
        ("pyproject.toml", lambda t: tomllib.loads(t).get("project")),
        ("package.json", lambda t: json.loads(t)),
        ("Cargo.toml", lambda t: tomllib.loads(t).get("package")),
    )
    for path, read in readers:
        text = git.blob(f"refs/tags/{tag}", path)
        if text is None:
            continue
        try:
            table = read(text)
        except (tomllib.TOMLDecodeError, json.JSONDecodeError):
            continue
        if isinstance(table, dict) and isinstance(table.get("name"), str) and table["name"]:
            description = table.get("description")
            return table["name"], description if isinstance(description, str) else ""
    return fallback_name, ""


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Sections, CHANGELOG.md and the job summary
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #


def section_heading(tag: str) -> re.Pattern[str]:
    return re.compile(rf"^## \[?{re.escape(tag)}\]?(\s|$)")


def section_of(text: str, tag: str) -> str | None:
    """The section for the tag, from its heading to the next "## " heading, or None."""
    heading = section_heading(tag)
    lines = text.splitlines()
    for i, line in enumerate(lines):
        if heading.match(line):
            end = next((j for j in range(i + 1, len(lines)) if lines[j].startswith("## ")), len(lines))
            return "\n".join(lines[i:end]).rstrip() + "\n"
    return None


def insert_section(text: str, section: str) -> str:
    """The section below "## [Unreleased]"; without that, below the title; else at the top."""
    m = re.search(r"^## \[Unreleased\][^\n]*(\n|$)", text, flags=re.MULTILINE) or re.search(
        r"^# [^\n]*(\n|$)", text, flags=re.MULTILINE
    )
    head, tail = (text[: m.end()], text[m.end() :]) if m else ("", text)
    head, tail = head.rstrip("\n"), tail.lstrip("\n")
    out = (head + "\n\n" if head else "") + section.rstrip("\n") + "\n"
    return out + ("\n" + tail if tail else "")


def assemble(tag: str, date: str, paragraph: str, listing: str) -> str:
    return f"## [{tag}] - {date}\n\n### Highlights\n\n{paragraph.strip()}\n\n{listing}\n"


def own_version() -> str:
    """dev-tools' version, from the VERSION.txt of the checkout this script runs from."""
    try:
        return (Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1] / "VERSION.txt").read_text(encoding="utf-8").strip() or "unknown"
    except OSError:
        return "unknown"


def literal(text: str) -> str:
    """Repository text for the job summary, as one Markdown code span on one line: every
    character that is not printable, line breaks among them, is shown as its escape, and
    the fence is one backtick longer than the longest run inside, so nothing in the text
    is read as Markdown or HTML."""
    text = "".join(c if c.isprintable() else c.encode("unicode_escape").decode("ascii") for c in text)
    if not text:
        return "(empty)"
    fence = "`" * (1 + max((len(run) for run in re.findall(r"`+", text)), default=0))
    return f"{fence} {text} {fence}"


def summary_markdown(repo: Repository, version: str, result: Result, written_by: str) -> str:
    out = [
        f"## {PROG}: {result.tag}",
        "",
        f"- dev-tools: {version}",
        f"- Repository: {repo.slug or repo.name}",
        f"- Range: `{result.range_text}`, {result.commit_count} commits",
        f"- Listed: {len(result.listed)} pull requests, {len(result.direct)} direct commits",
        f"- Highlights: {written_by}",
    ]

    def block(title: str, items: list[str]) -> None:
        out.extend(["", f"### {title}", ""])
        out.extend(items or ["- none"])

    block(
        "Listed pull requests",
        [
            f"- #{e.number}, {e.group}: "
            + "; ".join(
                f"{ROUTES[route]} {sha[:10]}" + (f" of {orig[:10]}" if route.startswith("cherry-pick") else "")
                for route, sha, orig in zip(e.routes, e.commits, _originals(e), strict=True)
            )
            + (f", absorbing {len(e.own)} own commits" if e.own else "")
            + (f" (breaking: {', '.join(e.breaking)})" if e.breaking else "")
            for e in result.listed
        ],
    )
    block("Direct commits", [f"- {d.sha[:7]} {literal(d.subject)}: {literal(d.note)}" for d in result.direct])
    block(
        "Left out",
        [f"- #{c.number}: {c.why}" for c in result.carriers] + [f"- #{s.number}: {s.why}" for s in result.shipped],
    )
    block("Bookkeeping", [f"- {b.sha[:7]} {literal(b.subject)}: {literal(b.reason)}" for b in result.bookkeeping])
    block("Warnings", [f"- {literal(w)}" for w in result.warnings])
    return "\n".join(out) + "\n"


def _originals(e: PullEntry) -> list[str]:
    """The original commit for each cherry-pick route, "" for the others, aligned with e.routes."""
    it = iter(e.originals)
    return [next(it, "") if route.startswith("cherry-pick") else "" for route in e.routes]


def emit_summary(markdown: str) -> None:
    """Print the report, and append it to the job summary when GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY is set.

    In a workflow the report goes between ::group:: markers, with workflow commands
    stopped around it, since titles and subjects are the repository's text, not the
    script's."""
    if os.environ.get("GITHUB_ACTIONS") == "true":
        token = os.urandom(16).hex()
        print(f"::group::{PROG} job summary")
        print(f"::stop-commands::{token}")
        print(markdown, end="")
        print(f"::{token}::")
        print("::endgroup::")
    else:
        print(markdown, end="")
    path = os.environ.get("GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY")
    if path:
        try:
            with open(path, "a", encoding="utf-8") as f:
                f.write(markdown)
        except OSError as e:
            print(f"{PROG}: warning: the job summary cannot be written: {e}", file=sys.stderr)


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# The modes
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #


def tag_from(explicit: str | None) -> str | None:
    """--tag, else the tag of a tag push's GITHUB_REF, else None."""
    if explicit is not None:
        if not TAG_RE.match(explicit):
            raise UsageError(f"--tag {explicit!r} is not a vX.Y.Z tag")
        return explicit
    ref = os.environ.get("GITHUB_REF", "")
    if ref.startswith("refs/tags/"):
        tag = ref.removeprefix("refs/tags/")
        if not TAG_RE.match(tag):
            raise UsageError(f"GITHUB_REF names the tag {tag!r}, which is not a vX.Y.Z tag")
        return tag
    return None


def generate(repo: Repository, tag: str, reuse_committed: bool) -> None:
    git = Git(repo.root)
    if git.run("rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", f"refs/tags/{tag}^{{commit}}", check=False).returncode != 0:
        raise UsageError(f"no tag {tag} in {repo.root}")
    version = own_version()
    print(f"{PROG} {version} (dev-tools), for {repo.slug or repo.name} {tag}")
    body_path = repo.root / RELEASE_BODY
    if reuse_committed:
        committed = git.blob("refs/remotes/origin/main", CHANGELOG)
        section = section_of(committed, tag) if committed is not None else None
        if section is not None:
            body_path.write_text(section, encoding="utf-8")
            print(
                f"generate: {CHANGELOG} on origin/main already holds the section for {tag}; "
                f"{RELEASE_BODY} is that section, unchanged, and no model call was made"
            )
            emit_summary(
                f"## {PROG}: {tag}\n\n- dev-tools: {version}\n- Repository: {repo.slug or repo.name}\n"
                f"- The section for {tag} was already in {CHANGELOG} on origin/main; {RELEASE_BODY} "
                "was written from it unchanged, with no model call.\n"
            )
            return
    prs = read_pull_requests(repo)
    result = build_list(git, prs, tag)
    name, description = project_meta(git, tag, repo.name)
    changes = highlights_input(highlight_entries(result, prs))
    paragraph, written_by = highlights(highlights_prompt(name, description, tag, result.prev, changes), result.warnings)
    date = git.out("log", "-1", "--format=%cs", f"refs/tags/{tag}").strip()
    body_path.write_text(assemble(tag, date, paragraph, result.render()), encoding="utf-8")
    print(
        f"generate: wrote {RELEASE_BODY} for {tag} (range {result.range_text}, {result.commit_count} commits): "
        f"{len(result.listed)} pull requests, {len(result.direct)} direct commits, "
        f"{len(result.bookkeeping)} bookkeeping commits left out"
    )
    emit_summary(summary_markdown(repo, version, result, written_by))
    if result.warnings:
        print(f"{PROG}: {len(result.warnings)} warnings, listed in the summary", file=sys.stderr)


def insert(repo: Repository, tag: str | None) -> None:
    body_path = repo.root / RELEASE_BODY
    if not body_path.is_file():
        raise UsageError(f"{RELEASE_BODY} not found in {repo.root}: run --mode=generate first")
    try:
        section = body_path.read_text(encoding="utf-8").rstrip() + "\n"
    except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError) as e:
        raise ChangelogError(f"{RELEASE_BODY} cannot be read: {e}") from None
    m = SECTION_HEAD_RE.match(section)
    if not m:
        raise ChangelogError(f"{RELEASE_BODY} does not begin with a '## [vX.Y.Z]' section heading")
    if tag is not None and m[1] != tag:
        raise ChangelogError(f"{RELEASE_BODY} holds the section for {m[1]}, not for {tag}")
    path = repo.root / CHANGELOG
    text = path.read_text(encoding="utf-8") if path.exists() else CHANGELOG_SKELETON
    if section_of(text, m[1]) is not None:
        print(f"insert: {CHANGELOG} already holds a section for {m[1]}; nothing to do")
        return
    path.write_text(insert_section(text, section), encoding="utf-8")
    print(f"insert: added the section for {m[1]} to {CHANGELOG}")


def main(argv: list[str] | None = None) -> int:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog=PROG, description="Generate a release's changelog section from its history and its pull requests."
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--mode",
        choices=("generate", "insert", "both"),
        default="both",
        help="generate writes release-body.md; insert adds it to CHANGELOG.md; both runs them in order",
    )
    parser.add_argument("--tag", metavar="vX.Y.Z", help="the release (default: the tag of GITHUB_REF)")
    parser.add_argument("--repo", metavar="DIR", default=".", help="the repository's root (default: .)")
    parser.add_argument(
        "--reuse-committed",
        action="store_true",
        help="generate: take the tag's section from CHANGELOG.md on origin/main when it is already there",
    )
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)
    try:
        repo = resolve_repository(args.repo)
        tag = tag_from(args.tag)
        if args.mode in ("generate", "both"):
            if tag is None:
                raise UsageError("no tag: pass --tag vX.Y.Z, or run on a tag push (GITHUB_REF=refs/tags/vX.Y.Z)")
            generate(repo, tag, args.reuse_committed)
        if args.mode in ("insert", "both"):
            insert(repo, tag)
    except UsageError as e:
        print(f"{PROG}: error: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    except ChangelogError as e:
        print(f"{PROG}: error: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
